ommunities leaving people trapped in  their homes with no way to get out at  least 10 people are dead and dozens more  are missing there's houses moved off  their foundation cars and trees i mean  this is uh this is almost the biblical  proportions here sometimes it's the  unnamed storms that do the most damage  as for already forecasters say the  maritimes will feel some wind and rain  but no major impacts when what's left of  the storm blows through in the next day  or two  ross lord global news  to haiti now where rescue teams are  still searching for survivors from last  weekend's devastating earthquake  mexican rescuers have now arrived in the  country to help sift through that debris  more than 2 000 people were killed in  the magnitude 7.2 quake and more than  300 people are still missing  ahead mong wan joe's extradition hearing  wraps up the ruling and the consequences  for canadians behind bars in china  the extradition hearing of chinese  telecom executive mungwon joe finally  concluded this past week in vancouver  nearly three years since her arrest it  marks a major step closer to a  conclusion for the high profile case  which could carry consequences far  beyond the courthouse  left the vancouver courthouse for what  could be one of the final times  the huawei executive's extradition  hearing wrapped up wednesday nearly  three years since her arrest rocked  canada china relations with beijing  detaining two canadians and sentencing  another to death  their fates as well as monks may now  rest in the hands of the bc judge the  chinese government has been cleared if  mrs mung is not returned to china there  will be no progress hmong was arrested  at the request of the united states and  accused of lying to hsbc back in 2013  about huawei's business dealings in iran  in violation of u.s sanctions  hmong's lawyers argued she never misled  the bank and the fraud charges are  purely political hmong won joe is a  political pawn she's been caught in the  middle of this u.s china trade fight and  that this case is about trade it's about  geopolitics it's about leverage but it's  not about justice hsbc suffered no  actual financial loss as a result but  the crown argued hmong put the british  bank at risk  this is unusual because the u.s has put  so much obviously so much effort into a  allegation of fraud that occurred  outside the united states not by an  american and not necessarily with an  american victim at times during the  hearing the judge sounded similarly  skeptical of the charges at one point  even asking the prosecution isn't it  unusual that one would see a fraud case  with no actual harm many years later and  one in which the alleged victim a large  institution appears to have numerous  people within the institution who had  all the facts that are now said to have  been misrepresented  well the questions that she asked her  and the comments that she made seem to  to  indicate that she's not impressed by the  strength of the evidence but impressed  or not the bar for extradition in canada  is low over the past decade canada has  only rejected about one percent of u.s  extradition requests  well it's of course much much lower than  a criminal trial it is to show that you  have the right person and there's enough  of a case there to warrant the surrender  the judge will set a date for her ruling  in october but the final decision will  then fall to canada's justice minister  if mung loses she's expected to appeal  that could see the case drag on for  years with canadians caught in the  middle  and you can hear much more on this and  other stories on my new podcast china  rising it explores the growing political  tensions between china canada and its  allies from pandemic disinformation to  hostage diplomacy subscribe for free now  wherever you get your podcasts  still ahead jugmeat sings pandemic  playbook a look inside the ndp campaign  amid covet  this election campaign is swapping  shaking hands and kissing babies with  elbow bumps and virtual town halls the  pandemic is changing how leaders get  their message in front of canadians  global news is going behind the scenes  with party leaders to see how they're  conducting the campaign in a time of  covid our michael couture is on the road  with jug meet singh's ndp and here's his  reporter's notebook from the campaign  trail  what a difference two years makes on day  one of the campaign jug meat singh  hopped on his chartered plane to  crisscross the country  compare that to 2019 it took nearly two  weeks for the ndp leader to leave his  bus tour of the greater toronto area  but while he's getting to more places  he can't necessarily get as close to  people because of the pandemic  19 has forced all leaders to adapt  the hugs and handshakes have been  replaced with elbow bumps and waves  it's a challenge for politicians like  singh who thrive in close quarters i  would love to be in big crowds and enjoy  that energy so we're going to have to do  things differently but we'll adapt oh my  goodness  and clearly adapting takes a bit of time  especially when singh's first event of  the campaign was marching in the  montreal pride parade where personal  space was at a premium it's forcing all  parties to conduct hybrid campaigns i  hereby declare his nomination meeting  which would have seen a huge rally in  his burnaby riding was instead virtual  from a vancouver hotel  stop lying to canadians jagmeet public  events do have some pitfalls as the ndp  learned the hard way in toronto hi  and singh still tries to get out and  connect with voters pounding the  pavement with his candidate looking for  votes  there has been one large crowd that was  in edmonton and singh clearly craves  more of it flower on the hands  despite the limits of pressing the flesh  singh is rolling forward hoping to flip  ridings that went to the conservatives  and liberals in 2019  [Music]  some party sources estimate they could  gain up to 12 new seats while others are  even more ambitious believing double  that number of writings are well within  reach  a far cry from the 2019 campaign when  the goal was to limit the damage and  limit the number of seats they knew they  were going to lose  mike liquiter global news toronto  here's a virtual tv elbow bump that is  global national for this sunday night  i'm jeff semple tonight's your canada is  mrs saggy river near sault ste marie  ontario thanks for watching donna  friesen we'll be back here with you  tomorrow have a great night  [Music]  you
 

